  {
    "episode_id": 43897,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/3/43897.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Look, I've made this case before. I'm in favor of shutting down the border and solving the absolute crisis we have going on in the United States right now. Mass deportation is going to pose a massive challenge for President Trump or the U.S. Congress and also fiscally for us. It is a whale of a fortune to do. However, it can be done systematically and in an orderly fashion, right? But here's a problem. A lot of these folks that are here, some of them actually have. Only about 9% have valid asylum claims. We are not about to start turning back our backs on people seeking refuge from tyrannical regimes all across the world. I'm not with that. However, for all the people who are taking advantage of our system and flooding our system with their nonsensical, non valid asylum claims, we should be sending them back. We should expand. The best way to have mass deportation is generally by increasing the immigration court system from 500 judges to 1,000 judges, because then the dockets will shrink and they will expedite these processes and they're going to be able to decline and actively engage these people in removal proceedings from the United States. So that would be the best solution and the most economical solution. And also, it would create an opportunity that at the border, the encounter should be you're detained for three to seven days within a processing center. And if that processing center is too full, nobody else is admitted. put them to the judge within seven days of decision comes back, boom, turn them right back. And if they're bound to be having a fraudulent claim of asylum by that judge, that person is barred. And if they tried to re enter enter the country, they don't even get a chance at the judge just automatically go back. So that's the best way is to creating no tolerance border policy. And it can be done.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker has previously made the argument for closing the border.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The U.S. should not reject people fleeing from tyrannical regimes worldwide.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Individuals making invalid or nonsensical asylum claims should be deported.",
